## 3. Misconception Registry

> Log of uncorrected assumptions surfaced during Socratic grilling. Append rows, never delete.
> A misconception is "resolved" only when I explain the correct model in my own words.

| ID | Concept | Misconception (what I believed) | Corrected model (my own words) | Status | Date |
|----|---------|---------------------------------|-------------------------------|--------|------|
| MIS-001 | <concept> | <what I got wrong> | <blank until resolved> | open | <YYYY-MM-DD> |

## 4. SM-2 Spaced Telemetry

> Per-concept spaced-repetition state. Extensions mutate these fields after each grill/recitation.
> Defaults: ease_factor 2.5, interval 0, repetitions 0. Reviews are scheduled on `next_review`.

| Concept | last_tested | next_review | interval (days) | ease_factor | repetitions |
|---------|-------------|-------------|-----------------|-------------|-------------|
| <concept-name> | — | — | 0 | 2.5 | 0 |

## Telemetry Contract

> Machine-readable updates. The grill/recitation skills emit JSON blocks like the one below at
> the end of a turn; downstream extensions parse them and write the fields back into this file.
> `status` is a bare emoji (🟥/🟨/🟩/🟦). `last_tested` and `next_review` are derived by the
> extension (last_tested = today, next_review = today + interval days). Keep keys stable.

```json
{
  "concept": "<concept-name>",
  "status": "🟨",
  "sm2": { "interval": 2, "ease_factor": 2.5, "repetitions": 1 },
  "misconception": { "id": "MIS-001", "description": "<the specific wrong assumption>", "status": "open" }
}
```
